<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Naser al-Din Shah Qajar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1831">1831</a>–<a href="/years/1896">1896</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Naser al-Din Shah was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> shah of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> from 1848 to 1896, the longest reign of the dynasty. His minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir-kabir">Amir Kabir</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dar-ul-funun">Dar ol-Fonun</a> college, and the shah travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, but he relied on foreign loans and concessions, provoking the tobacco protest of 1891–1892. He centralised power, ending Kurdish autonomy in Ardalan in 1867, and was assassinated in 1896.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/naser-al-din-shah-qajar.jpg" alt="Naser al-Din Shah Qajar" width="320" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nadar / Adam Cuerden · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Naser_al-Din_Shah_Qajar,_close_up,_with_slight_smile_by_Nadar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
